w: For God, the Source of ev- ery- thing, Thy Por- tion shall re- main. 
w: Thy Coun- se- lor when doubts an- noy, Thy Shield and great Re- ward. 
w: On thy Cre- a- tor cast thy care; He makes thy bur- dens light. 
w: Has He not oft in threat- 'ning hour Turned dread- ed ills a- way? 
w: All that His coun- sels did or- dain A hap- py end- ing makes. 
w: Then like a rock thy peace shall stand Here and in heav'n a- bove.
w: To Earth Thy know- ledge I im- part, As it is known to me.
w: Whence sa- ving health and good- ness flow Each day so boun- teou- sly.
w: That does not from Thy boun- teous love, O Heav'n- ly Fa- ther flow?
w: By whom are to our pas- tures sent Re- fresh- ing rain and dew?
w: Who or- ders it that oil and must We in their sea- son find?
w: In gol- den peace, wards off war's woes From our dear na- tive land?
w: Thou kee- pest watch be- fore our door, While we se- cure- ly rest.
w: With rea- dy help in time of fear, Thou stan- dest at our side.
w: And all thy sins He casts a- side And in the sea doth drown.
w: Sup- plies what to His glo- ry tends And to thine own re- lief.
w: And in the book His mer- cy keeps These things are not- ed all.
w: Thou lead'st us to our home on high, When hence we pass a- way.
